<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ingush people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ingush are a Nakh people of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, living mainly in the Republic of Ingushetia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Organised in clans in mountain villages with stone towers, they adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> largely in the 18th and 19th centuries. In 1944 Stalin deported them with the Chechens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and their return in 1957 left a territorial dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ingush-people.jpg" alt="Ingush people" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingush_Highlanders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
